1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,784
The school plan names a 220-kilogram adult on a third floor with
symptomatic bradycardia. Treat that as a scenario brief, not as enough
information to choose a treatment or assume the patient must be moved
immediately.

2
00:00:14,784 --> 00:00:33,480
Your first task is to recognize immediate threats, obtain an accurate
picture of the patient, request the right level of help and
communicate uncertainty. Apply the current authorized pathway and
instructor direction; this rehearsal supplies no medication dose or
independent treatment order.

3
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:48,576
Say “the patient who weighs 220 kilograms” or use the patient’s
preferred language, rather than using weight as the person’s identity.
Explain why a measurement or movement is being considered and ask
permission where appropriate.

4
00:00:48,576 --> 00:01:03,936
Dignity and safety are compatible. Discuss staffing and equipment
needs privately and matter-of-factly; do not promise a transfer method
until the actual load limits, patient condition, trained personnel and
route are confirmed.

5
00:01:03,936 --> 00:01:18,000
A slow pulse on a screen is a finding, not a complete diagnosis.
Confirm the signal and compare it with the patient’s presentation,
pulse quality, symptoms, mental status and other available
measurements.

6
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:34,608
Use the current PCP pathway and monitor instructions for the
assessment and any intervention. If the patient deteriorates or falls
outside your authorization, escalate promptly and communicate what
changed rather than improvising a medication plan.

7
00:01:34,608 --> 00:01:58,632
The school brief names symptomatic bradycardia but does not provide a
rhythm strip, blood pressure, drug history or a completed examination.
Obtain those findings in the actual station rather than inventing
them. Connect rate with stroke volume and perfusion, and describe the
evidence that makes the situation urgent instead of assuming that one
rate value defines stability.

8
00:01:58,632 --> 00:02:22,584
Use the CV and A&P routes to explain competing mechanisms, then use
current authorized assessment and escalation for the actual
presentation. A conduction finding, a medication effect and an
ischaemic problem can overlap. Do not manufacture an ECG diagnosis, a
pacing indication or a drug order from this short educational brief.

9
00:02:22,584 --> 00:02:47,880
Ask what the patient actually took and when, including OTC and
complementary products, rather than copying an old medicine list. A
nodal-slowing drug, pressure treatment or sedating exposure may
matter, but the case brief does not establish any of them. Treat these
as questions and competing explanations until the patient, packaging
or another reliable source supplies the facts.

10
00:02:47,880 --> 00:03:12,096
The PHARM routes connect target, intended effect, accumulation,
interaction and adverse response. They do not authorize stopping
chronic therapy or giving a reversal medicine on suspicion alone. Keep
immediate patient support, current service direction and consultation
ahead of a speculative interaction story, and communicate the exposure
timeline with its uncertainty.

11
00:03:12,096 --> 00:03:25,560
For blood pressure, use the manufacturer’s cuff range and the actual
measured arm circumference; do not assume that the largest available
cuff fits. Poor fit or placement can make a number misleading.

12
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:44,592
For oxygen saturation, assess signal quality and whether the
measurement makes sense alongside the patient’s presentation. If the
usual site is unsuitable, use an alternate site only when the specific
probe instructions and service guidance approve it; record the site
and any limitation.

13
00:03:44,592 --> 00:04:08,280
For an original variation, suppose a repeated pressure looks better
while the patient becomes less responsive. This variation is not an
extra fact from the school case. Check whether the measurement methods
are comparable, but do not let the improved number overrule the
deteriorating patient. Reassess immediate threats and communicate the
new concern through the actual pathway.

14
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:29,184
FON helps you update the hypothesis when new cues appear, and MSN asks
whether the expected outcome actually occurred. State what improved,
what worsened, what is still unknown and what you will reassess next.
Do not automatically repeat a previous action or declare success
because one task was completed.

15
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:44,760
The school specifically expects you to plan equipment capacity,
staffing and the exit route before movement. Include the entire
system—device, accessories, attachment points, surface and the
route—not just a label on one stretcher.

16
00:04:44,760 --> 00:05:02,304
A third-floor route adds stairs, turns, door widths, landings and
limited ability to stop. If capacity, trained staff or a safe route is
uncertain, pause, protect the patient and request the appropriate
resources instead of attempting an improvised lift.

17
00:05:02,304 --> 00:05:27,336
Before committing to the third-floor move, reconcile the patient’s
current needs with the route, trained assistance and the complete
equipment system. The patient weighs two hundred twenty kilograms, but
safe capacity is not established by adding helpers or checking only
one device. Explain the plan respectfully, preserve dignity and
arrange the resources the actual situation requires.

18
00:05:27,336 --> 00:05:47,904
Use a receiver check-back so the person assigned a task states what
they understood, and confirm that understanding before movement. Agree
who watches the patient, what change stops the move and how the team
will respond. A perfect equipment plan that loses airway access or
misses deterioration is not a complete patient plan.

19
00:05:47,904 --> 00:06:02,280
The saved plan says the second case should be personalized from an
individual Appendix G weakness. Because the official form was not in
the saved materials, this lesson cannot know or invent the learner’s
actual priority.

20
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:17,856
Once the instructor or learner has the real priority, turn it into one
observable behavior and practise it in a short original case. Change
one cue on the next run so the learner must transfer the skill instead
of memorizing a script.

21
00:06:17,856 --> 00:06:42,624
The second school case is based on the learner’s actual gap; no such
individual record is available here. Select the relevant reading only
after identifying the observed weakness. If the difficulty involves
communication, use the PMHN case method: describe the words and
behaviour, ask one open question, listen and check meaning without
assigning a stigmatizing label.

22
00:06:42,624 --> 00:07:05,952
Psychiatric history does not exclude a simultaneous medical cause of
altered behaviour or consciousness. Continue indicated assessment and
report objective changes alongside the patient’s account. Change one
case cue at a time and observe whether the targeted behaviour
transfers, rather than calling a generic scenario a personalized
remediation plan.

23
00:07:05,952 --> 00:07:22,368
The school plan asks for one or two system scenarios, extensive
documentation and a formal handover. Follow the actual station brief;
do not add hidden scoring rules or assume that a provincial licensing
standard is the school’s standard.

24
00:07:22,368 --> 00:07:37,488
In the debrief, separate the observed action from the interpretation.
For example: “The repeat value was not stated in the handover” is
observable; “I am bad at communication” is a broad judgment that does
not identify a drill.

25
00:07:37,488 --> 00:07:50,736
A useful record distinguishes the patient’s report, your direct
observation, monitor data and information from another source. Include
the timing and response associated with each important action.

26
00:07:50,736 --> 00:08:08,688
Keep the verbal handover concise but complete enough to support
continuity: who the patient is, why care is needed, what changed, what
was done, what response followed and what remains unresolved. Use the
current service format and confirm transfer of responsibility.
